message:
Now I see it. `buf[0] = (char)plen` where `plen` can be up to 255. If `plen > 127`, then `buf[0]` becomes negative when interpreted as a signed char. Then in vm.c:

```c
regs[a] = mrb_bint_new_str(mrb, s+2, (mrb_int)s[0], (mrb_int)s[1]);
```

`(mrb_int)s[0]` would be negative (e.g., -56 for plen=200). Then in `mpz_init_set_str`, `len = -56`, and the loop `for (i = len-1;; i--)` starts with `i = -57`, reading `s[-57]` which is out of bounds.

But wait - the `new_litbn` function checks `if (plen > 255) codegen_error(s, "integer too big")`. So `plen` can be up to 255. For `plen = 128` to `255`, `buf[0]` as signed char would be `-128` to `-1`.

But there's also the `mrb_assert(2 <= iabs(base) && iabs(base) <= 36)` in `mrb_bint_new_str`. For a positive bigint, `base = s[1]` which is the actual base (2-36). For a negative bigint, `base = 0x80 = -128`, so `iabs(-128) = 128 > 36`, which would fail the assert. But since `MRB_DEBUG` is not defined in the fuzzer build, the assert is a no-op.

So the bug is: a bigint literal with more than 127 digits causes `s[0]` (the length byte) to be negative, leading to a negative `len` in `mpz_init_set_str`, which causes an out-of-bounds read.

But wait - the task says "heap-use-after-free". Let me think about this more carefully. The out-of-bounds read with negative index would read before the string buffer. But the string buffer is allocated with `codegen_realloc(s, NULL, plen+3)`. Reading before it could hit freed memory.

Actually, let me reconsider. The `s` in vm.c points to the pool data. The pool is part of the irep structure. Reading `s[-57]` would read before the pool string, potentially into other pool data or freed memory.

But actually, let me re-read the loop in `mpz_init_set_str`:

```c
for (i = len-1;; i--) {
    if (s[i]=='_') continue;
    if (s[i] >= '0' && s[i] <= '9')
      k = (unsigned int)s[i] - (unsigned int)'0';
    ...
    if (k >= base) {
      retval = (-1);
      break;
    }
    ...
    if (i == 0) break;
}
```

With `len = -56`, `i` starts at `-57`. `s[-57]` reads 57 bytes before the string. This is an out-of-bounds read. If the byte happens to be a valid digit and `k < base`, it continues. The loop goes `i = -57, -58, -59, ...` and never reaches `i == 0` (it goes more negative). So it keeps reading out of bounds until it hits a non-digit or `k >= base`.

This is a heap-buffer-overflow read (reading before the buffer), which could be a heap-use-after-free if the memory before the buffer was freed.

Now, how do we trigger this? We need a bigint literal with more than 127 digits. Let me check how bigint literals are parsed in the compiler:

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<｜｜DSML｜｜=command>grep -n "new_litbn" /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/codegen.c | head -10</｜｜DSML｜｜>
</｜｜DSML｜｜>

